According to local converse theorem definitions by Friedberg and the author, two irreducible admissible representations are isomorphic if and only they share the same central character and have equivalent local gamma factors (potentially for a subset of $n \geq 1$). However, this paper demonstrates that to uniquely determine an irreducible generic representation solely from its local gamma factors (even when restricted to $n \leq 2r-1$), one additional datum—the central character of the representation—is necessary. Consequently, the converse theorem for Shahidi's local gamma factors was updated for special orthogonal groups (from $n \leq 2r-3$ to $n \leq 2r+1$), and a significantly higher bound was achieved for symplectic groups (from $n \leq 2r-1$ to $n \leq 4r+1$).

